k:break-word;\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">Great question! In physics, when figuring out <strong>net force<\/strong> (the overall force acting on an object), the calculations often <strong>use vectors<\/strong>, which are usually represented as <strong>arrows<\/strong> in diagrams or as <strong>lines with direction and magnitude<\/strong>.<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">When it comes to equations and adding forces together, the \"shapes\" they use are often:<\/p><ol style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Straight Line (Linear\/Collinear Forces):<\/strong><ul style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">If all the forces act in a straight line (like a tug-of-war), you just add or subtract the forces (with direction as positive or negative).<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">Shape used: <strong>straight line<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Parallelogram or Triangle (Non-Collinear Forces):<\/strong><ul style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">If the forces act at angles to each other, you often <strong>draw them as arrows from a common point<\/strong> and then use the <strong>parallelogram law<\/strong> or the <strong>triangle law<\/strong> to add vectors.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">The most common shapes here are:<ul style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Triangle<\/strong> (triangle law of vector addition)<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Parallelogram<\/strong> (parallelogram law of vector addition)<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ol><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>Summary Table:<\/strong><\/p><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin:8px 0 12px 0;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Situation<\/th><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Shape Used<\/th><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Description<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces in one direction<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Straight Line<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces added\/subtracted linearly<\/td><\/tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces at angles<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Parallelogram<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Combine using parallelogram law<\/td><\/tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces at angles<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Triangle<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Combine using triangle law<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>In Equations:<\/strong><br>The net force is often written as:<\/p><pre style=\"margin:8px 0 12px 0; padding:12px; background:#f6f8fa; border:1px solid #d0d7de; border-radius:6px; white-space:pre-wrap; overflow-wrap:anywhere; word-break:break-word;\"><code>\\vec{F}_{\\text{net}} = \\vec{F}_1 + \\vec{F}_2 + \\vec{F}_3 + \\cdots<\/code><\/pre><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">This equation implies you are <strong>adding vectors<\/strong>, which are usually represented using the shapes above.<\/p><hr style=\"margin:12px 0; border:none; border-top:1px solid #d0d7de;\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>In short:<\/strong><br>They use <strong>lines, triangles, or parallelograms<\/strong> to represent and add forces in net force equations, depending on direction!<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">If you want a diagram or example for a specific situation, just ask!<\/p><\/div>","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"DB","Selector":"TB","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText"},"QuestionDescription":"Response A: Great question! In physics, when figuring out net force (the overall force acting on...","ChoiceOrder":[],"Validation":{"Settings":{"Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":4,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionText_Unsafe":"<p><strong>Response B:<\/strong><\/p><div style=\"width:100%; max-width:100%; overflow-wrap:anywhere; word-break:break-word;\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">Great question! In physics, when figuring out <strong>net force<\/strong> (the overall force acting on an object), the calculations often <strong>use vectors<\/strong>, which are usually represented as <strong>arrows<\/strong> in diagrams or as <strong>lines with direction and magnitude<\/strong>.<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">When it comes to equations and adding forces together, the \"shapes\" they use are often:<\/p><ol style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Straight Line (Linear\/Collinear Forces):<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">If all the forces act in a straight line (like a tug-of-war), you just add or subtract the forces (with direction as positive or negative).<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">Shape used: <strong>straight line<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Parallelogram or Triangle (Non-Collinear Forces):<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">If the forces act at angles to each other, you often <strong>draw them as arrows from a common point<\/strong> and then use the <strong>parallelogram law<\/strong> or the <strong>triangle law<\/strong> to add vectors.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">The most common shapes here are:<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Triangle<\/strong> (triangle law of vector addition)<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Parallelogram<\/strong> (parallelogram law of vector addition)<\/li><\/ul><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>Summary Table:<\/strong><\/p><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin:8px 0 12px 0;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Situation<\/th><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Shape Used<\/th><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Description<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces in one direction<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Straight Line<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces added\/subtracted linearly<\/td><\/tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces at angles<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Parallelogram<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Combine using parallelogram law<\/td><\/tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces at angles<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Triangle<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Combine using triangle law<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>In Equations:<\/strong><br>The net force is often written as:<\/p><pre style=\"margin:8px 0 12px 0; padding:12px; background:#f6f8fa; border:1px solid #d0d7de; border-radius:6px; white-space:pre-wrap; overflow-wrap:anywhere; word-break:break-word;\"><code>\\vec{F}_{\\text{net}} = \\vec{F}_1 + \\vec{F}_2 + \\vec{F}_3 + \\cdots<\/code><\/pre><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">This equation implies you are <strong>adding vectors<\/strong>, which are usually represented using the shapes above.<\/p><hr style=\"margin:12px 0; border:none; border-top:1px solid #d0d7de;\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>In short:<\/strong><br>They use <strong>lines, triangles, or parallelograms<\/strong> to represent and add forces in net force equations, depending on direction!<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">If you want a diagram or example for a specific situation, just ask!<\/p><\/div>","DataExportTag":"P03_V2_ResponseA","QuestionID":"QID137","QuestionJS":false}},{"SurveyID":"SV_7UHFd5UMl2pr8UK","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID138","SecondaryAttribute":"In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its c...","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<strong>In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its content and the way it is written. There are no right or wrong answers, we are interested in your honest judgment.<\/strong>","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"DB","Selector":"TB","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText"},"QuestionDescription":"In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its c...","ChoiceOrder":[],"Validation":{"Settings":{"Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":4,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionText_Unsafe":"<strong>In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its content and the way it is written. There are no right or wrong answers, we are interested in your honest judgment.<\/strong>","DataExportTag":"P03_Tones_B","QuestionID":"QID138"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_7UHFd5UMl2pr8UK","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID139","SecondaryAttribut